# Code owners (docs/m4/plan.md, REL; narrowed on 2026-11-11, owner). Only the files that decide what a
# release is, or who may sign it, need the owner's review, not every PR. This takes effect only with
# the "require review from Code Owners" setting of the main ruleset, which is the owner's to turn on.
#
# The last pattern that matches a file wins, and a pattern with no owner leaves it unowned. So the
# directories below are owned as a whole and the few files that need no owner are named after them:
# a NEW file in them (a release script, a workflow that could ask for a token or a secret) is owned
# until someone unowns it here, which itself needs the owner.
/.github/CODEOWNERS @anthonytw
/.github/release-signers @anthonytw
/.github/package-signing-key.asc @anthonytw
/.github/macos-team-id @anthonytw
/.github/workflows/** @anthonytw
/tests/release/** @anthonytw
/Cross.toml @anthonytw
/rust-toolchain.toml @anthonytw
# rustup prefers this name over rust-toolchain.toml when both exist; listed so that adding it needs the owner.
/rust-toolchain @anthonytw
# What else decides what a release is: the workspace manifest (profile, version, patches, and cross's
# metadata), the dependency and notice policy and its template, cargo and git settings that would
# reach the builds, the packaging templates, the Nix flake (the Nix channel's build), and the man
# pages' toolchain, build and pandoc filter (docs/m4/plan.md, DOC). The release path's recipes are
# tests/release/release.just (owned above), which reads no other file: release.yml,
# the release scripts and the owner run it through tests/release/just.sh, never the top-level justfile
# (tests/release/recipes.sh, and lint-workflow.sh's rule 9).
/Cargo.toml @anthonytw
/deny.toml @anthonytw
/about.toml @anthonytw
/about.hbs @anthonytw
/.cargo/ @anthonytw
/.gitattributes @anthonytw
/packaging/** @anthonytw
/flake.nix @anthonytw
/flake.lock @anthonytw
/docs/man/** @anthonytw
# Unowned. The justfile (nothing on the release path reads it, above). The manual check workflows use
# no secret and ask for no write permission (contents and actions: read only), which the owned
# lint-workflow.sh --check requires, run by release.yml's prepare job and by the owned `release-test`
# recipe before selftest.sh; a dispatch on a ci/ branch runs that branch's copy of them anyway. The
# release scripts' own tests: release.yml runs them in a job of their own whose writes reach no
# other job, and they build nothing that ships. And the fil(1) page, which is text.
/.github/workflows/linux-check.yml
/.github/workflows/macos-check.yml
/tests/release/selftest.sh
/tests/release/selftest_macho.py
/tests/release/selftest_pkg.py
/docs/man/fil.1
